WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.379
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:08.690
We have recently witnessed the emergence of
neural network-based techniques that are able

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.179
to synthesize all sorts of images.

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:16.830
Our previous episode was about NVIDIA's algorithm
that created high resolution images of imaginary

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.980
celebrities that was a really cool application
of Generative Adversarial Networks.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.600
This architecture means that we have a pair
of neural networks, one that learns to generate

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.290
new images, and the other learns to tell a
fake image from a real one.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:36.059
As they compete against each other, they get
better and better without any human interaction.

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:40.499
So we can clearly use them to create 2D images,
but why stop there?

00:00:40.499 --> 00:00:45.050
Why not use this technique, for instance,
to create assets for digital media?

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:50.070
So instead of 2D images, let's try to adapt
these networks to generate high-resolution

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.879
3D models of terrains that we can use to populate
a virtual world.

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:59.199
Both computer games and the motion picture
industry could benefit greatly from such a

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:00.199
tool.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:05.840
This process is typically done via procedural
generation, which is basically a sort of guided

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.550
random terrain generation.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.170
Here, we can have a more direct effect on
the output without putting in tens of hours

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.140
of work to get the job done.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.070
In the first training step, this technique
learns how an image of a terrain corresponds

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.640
to input drawings.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:27.070
Then, we will be able to sketch a draft of
a landscape with rivers, ridges, valleys and

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:31.450
the algorithm will output a high quality model
of the terrain itself.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.820
During this process, we can have a look at
the current output and refine our drawings

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.230
in the meantime, leading to a super efficient
process where we can go from a thought to

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:46.590
a high-quality final result within a few seconds
without being bogged down with the technical

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:47.590
details.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:53.620
What's more, it can also not only deal with
erased subregions, but it can also automatically

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.210
fill them with sensible information to save
time for us.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.540
What an outstanding convenience feature!

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:05.820
And, the algorithm can also perform physical
manipulations like erosion to the final results.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.769
After the training for the erosion step is
done, the computational cost is practically

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:16.430
zero, for instance, running an erosion simulator
on this piece of data would take around 40

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:21.370
seconds, where the neural network can do it
in 25 milliseconds.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.249
The full simulation would almost be a minute,
where the network can mimic its results practically

00:02:26.249 --> 00:02:27.249
instantaneously.

00:02:27.249 --> 00:02:33.319
A limitation of this technique is that if
the input is too sparse, unpleasant grid artifacts

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:34.319
may appear.

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:39.099
There are tons of more cool features in the
paper, make sure to have a look, as always,

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:40.769
it is available in the video description.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:46.889
This is a really well thought out and well-presented
work that I expect to be a true powerhouse

00:02:46.889 --> 00:02:48.780
for terrain authoring in the future.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.780
And, in the meantime, we have reached a hundred
thousand subscribers.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.590
A hundred thousand Fellow Scholars.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:56.590
Wow.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:01.560
This is absolutely amazing and honestly I
never thought that this would ever happen.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.529
So, happy paperversary, thank you very much
for coming along on this journey of science

00:03:06.529 --> 00:03:11.450
and I am very happy to see that the series
brings joy and learning to more people than

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:12.450
ever.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.760
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

